.\" xping manual page
.\" Created by Mehdi Askari <iorganamis@gmail.com>
.TH XPING 1 "2026-09-25" "xping 1.4.1" "Network Diagnostics"
.
.SH NAME
xping \- beautiful CLI network diagnostics
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xping
.RI [ \-V ]
.I COMMAND
.I HOST
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.PP
.B xping
.I HOST
.RS
(shorthand: auto-pings HOST and shows next-step suggestions)
.RE
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xping
is a command-line network diagnostics tool that provides live,
colour-coded, animated output for ICMP ping, traceroute, MTR,
DNS lookup, TLS inspection, HTTP diagnostics, WHOIS, port scanning,
IP discovery, network health scoring, speed testing, local port
listing, OS fingerprinting, and more.
.PP
Each packet and each hop is displayed the moment it is received \(em
results are never buffered and shown all at once.
True-colour ANSI graphics, animated spinners, latency bars,
sparkline charts, and histogram distributions make network behaviour
instantly readable.
.PP
.B xping
speaks ICMP and ICMPv6 natively.
On macOS and Linux it uses unprivileged ICMP ("ping") sockets, so
.BR ping ,
.BR trace ,
and
.B mtr
work without root; raw sockets are used when running as root, and the
system
.BR ping (8)
and
.BR traceroute (8)
binaries are the last fallback.
IPv4 is preferred, IPv6-only hosts work automatically, and
.B \-4
/
.B \-6
force a family.
.PP
Every command exits 0 when its check passes and 1 when it fails, and
most accept thresholds (for example
.BR \-\-max\-loss ,
.BR \-\-max\-latency ,
.BR \-\-min\-days ),
so xping can be used from scripts, cron and monitoring systems; see
.B EXIT STATUS
and
.BR "SCRIPTING AND MONITORING" .
It requires only the
.B certifi
package (auto-installed); all other functionality uses the Python
standard library (Python \(>= 3.10).
.PP
Running
.B xping HOST
without a subcommand auto-pings the target and then prints a
list of suggested follow-up commands.
.PP
Created by
.B Mehdi Askari
.RI < iorganamis@gmail.com >.
Any fork or redistribution must visibly credit the original author
per the license terms.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Basic diagnostics
.TP
.BR ping " \fIHOST\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Send ICMP echo requests to
.IR HOST .
Each reply is printed immediately showing a colour-coded latency bar
and RTT value. The summary reports sent/received counts, packet loss,
min/avg/max/jitter RTT, a sparkline timeline, and a latency histogram.
Use
.B \-\-watch
for continuous live mode with running statistics (Ctrl-C to stop).
.TP
.BR trace " \fIHOST\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Perform a hop-by-hop traceroute to
.IR HOST .
Each hop is printed in real time. RTT values are colour-coded:
green < 30 ms, amber < 100 ms, yellow < 300 ms, red otherwise.
The summary includes total hops, responding hops and final-hop RTT.
Probes are hop-limited ICMP echoes (no root needed on macOS/Linux);
.B \-\-tcp
uses TCP SYNs to a real service port instead, which gets through
firewalls that drop ICMP;
.B \-\-asn
adds each hop's network operator.
.TP
.BR mtr " \fIHOST\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Combined live traceroute and per-hop ping (My Traceroute).
Discovers the path to
.IR HOST ,
then pings every hop repeatedly and redraws a live table showing
per-hop loss%, last/avg/best/worst RTT, standard deviation, and a
sparkline.
.B \-\-asn
adds an AS number/operator column.
Falls back to the system
.B traceroute
when no ICMP socket can be opened.
.TP
.BR lookup " \fIHOST\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Query DNS records for
.IR HOST :
A (IPv4), AAAA (IPv6), CNAME, MX mail servers, NS nameservers,
and optionally TXT records (e.g. SPF). DMARC and DKIM live on
separate names; use
.B dnscheck
for those.
Reverse-DNS hostnames are resolved for every address found.
Use
.B \-\-server IP
to query a specific resolver (e.g.
.BR \-\-server\ 8.8.8.8 )
like
.BR "dig @8.8.8.8" .
Falls back to raw UDP DNS when
.B dig
is absent.
.TP
.BR dnscheck " \fIDOMAIN\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Full DNS health check for
.IR DOMAIN :
A/AAAA records, NS redundancy, MX backup, SPF policy (strict/soft/none),
DMARC policy (reject/quarantine/none) from
.IR _dmarc.DOMAIN ,
and DKIM key presence at common selectors
.RI ( selector ._domainkey. DOMAIN ).
Reports a 0-100 score with pass/warn/fail per check.
Checks whose DNS query failed (e.g. SERVFAIL or timeout) are marked
as not verified and excluded from the score.
.TP
.BR rdns " \fIIP\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Reverse-DNS (PTR) lookup for
.IR IP .
Queries the system resolver first; falls back to a direct UDP query
to 8.8.8.8 when the system resolver has no PTR support.
.TP
.BR tcp " \fIHOST\fR \fIPORT\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Open TCP connections to
.I HOST
on
.IR PORT .
Each attempt is printed immediately with open/failed status and
connect timing. The summary reports success rate and min/avg/max times.
.B \-\-watch
and
.B \-\-until\-up
repeat the check (see
.BR "WATCH MODE" ).
.TP
.BR tls " \fIHOST\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Inspect the TLS/SSL certificate presented by
.I HOST
on port 443 (or
.BR \-\-port ).
Shows protocol version, cipher suite, subject, issuer, SAN list,
expiry date, days remaining, a lifetime progress bar, and (on Python
3.13+) the full certificate chain from leaf to root CA.
.TP
.BR http " \fIURL\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Perform an HTTP GET to
.IR URL ,
following redirects automatically.
Reports the status code, redirect chain, response headers, the HTTP
version used, whether the server supports HTTP/2 (detected via ALPN),
the TLS version and cipher, and a per-phase timing waterfall for the
final request: DNS lookup \(md TCP connect \(md TLS handshake \(md
server response (time to first byte) \(md download, plus the time spent
on redirects and the total.
A security-header audit checks HTTPS (and an http\(->https upgrade),
.B Strict\-Transport\-Security
(max-age of at least 180 days),
.BR Content\-Security\-Policy ,
.BR "X\-Content\-Type\-Options: nosniff" ,
.B X\-Frame\-Options
(or CSP frame-ancestors),
.BR Referrer\-Policy ,
.BR Permissions\-Policy ,
and
.BR Server / X\-Powered\-By
headers that reveal software versions; it is informational only.
Exits 1 on HTTP status \(>= 400 unless
.B \-\-expect\-status
says otherwise.
.TP
.BR propagation " \fINAME\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Query
.I NAME
on your system resolver and on Google, Cloudflare, Quad9, OpenDNS,
AdGuard and Control D in parallel, and show which answers differ from
the majority \(em the quick way to see whether a DNS change has
propagated.
Different answers alone do not fail the command (CDNs and geo-DNS
vary on purpose);
.B \-\-expect
makes it exit 1 unless every answering resolver returns the value.
.TP
.BR whois " \fIDOMAIN\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Look up WHOIS registration data for
.IR DOMAIN .
Queries the authoritative registry WHOIS server on port 43, following
up to two referrals (IANA \(-> registry \(-> registrar), with up to two
retries per server. Falls back automatically to RDAP over HTTPS when
port 43 is blocked or firewalled.
.SS Network scanning
.TP
.BR portscan " \fIHOST\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Scan TCP ports on
.IR HOST
concurrently.
Use
.B \-\-banners
to grab service banners from open ports (SSH version, HTTP server,
FTP greeting, Redis ping, PostgreSQL greeting, etc.).
.TP
.BR ipscan " \fIRANGE\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Discover live hosts in a CIDR block or start-end IP range using ICMP
echo probes through the system
.BR ping (8)
binary.
.TP
.BR sweep " \fIRANGE\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Probe a CIDR block or start-end IP range for hosts with open TCP ports.
Useful for finding HTTP servers or SSH gateways on a local network.
.TP
.BR mtu " \fIHOST\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Discover the Path MTU to
.I HOST
via binary search using DF-flagged (Don't Fragment) ping probes.
Identifies the largest unfragmented packet size along the path;
useful for diagnosing VPN or tunnel fragmentation issues.
IPv6 is supported on Linux and macOS (macOS needs root for the
don't-fragment IPv6 probe) but not on Windows.
.SS Local machine
.TP
.BR doctor " [\fIHOST\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Answer \(lqwhy is the internet not working?\(rq.
Checks, in order: a network interface with a usable address
(a 169.254.x.x address means DHCP failed), the default gateway
(ping), the internet by IP address without DNS (TCP 443 to 1.1.1.1,
8.8.8.8 and 9.9.9.9), DNS through the system resolver (on failure, a
direct query to 1.1.1.1 tells a broken resolver apart from blocked DNS
traffic), connection quality (loss and latency to 1.1.1.1), a captive
portal (Wi-Fi login page, via captive.apple.com), HTTPS with certificate
verification (interception; Cloudflare's timestamp also reveals a wrong
system clock), IPv6 (informational only) and, when given,
.I HOST
on the TCP port set with
.BR \-\-port .
Steps that depend on a failed one are skipped.
It ends with one plain-language diagnosis and what to do.
Exits 1 when any step fails.
.TP
.B net [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
Your own network at a glance: hostname, the local IPv4/IPv6 source
addresses used for outbound traffic, default gateways, DNS resolvers,
public IPv4/IPv6 addresses (with country and Cloudflare data centre),
and interfaces with state, MTU and addresses.
The public-address lookup is the only outbound request
.RI ( https://1.1.1.1/cdn-cgi/trace )
and is skipped with
.BR \-\-no\-public .
.TP
.B listen [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
List locally listening TCP/UDP ports, with process names and PIDs
where the platform reports them (Linux).
Uses
.BR ss (8)
on Linux,
.BR netstat (8)
on macOS and Windows.
Filter with
.B \-\-proto tcp
or
.BR \-\-proto udp .
.TP
.BR osdetect " \fIHOST\fR"
Guess the operating system of a remote host by analysing the
TTL value in the ICMP echo reply.
Common signatures: TTL\~\(>=\~114 and \(<=\~128 \(-> Windows;
TTL\~\(>=\~57 and \(<=\~64 \(-> Linux/macOS/FreeBSD;
TTL\~\(>=\~241 \(-> Linux kernel/Cisco IOS/Solaris.
This is a heuristic \(em VPNs and tunnels can change the observed TTL.
.TP
.B speedtest
Measure latency, download and upload throughput using Cloudflare's
public speed endpoint
.RI ( speed.cloudflare.com ).
Uses 4 parallel HTTPS connections by default (a single stream
under-reports fast links); download runs for at most
.B \-\-duration
seconds and is timed from the first byte, upload is timed after the TLS
handshakes. Data used: up to about 40 MB down and 8 MB up
.RB ( "\-c 1" :
25 MB down).
No
.B iperf3
required.
.SS Health and profiles
.TP
.BR health " \fIHOST\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Measure DNS resolve time, packet loss, average latency, and jitter
for
.I HOST
and compute a 0-100 Network Health Score
(Excellent / Good / Fair / Poor / Critical).
Each subsequent run is saved to
.IR ~/.xping/health_history.json ;
the score history and trend arrow are displayed on every run.
.TP
.B profile \fISUBCOMMAND\fR
Manage saved target profiles stored in
.IR ~/.xping/profiles.json .
Any command that accepts a HOST transparently resolves a profile name
to its stored target.
.RS
.TP
.BI "add " "NAME TARGET " "[\-\-port \fIN\fR] [\-\-note \fITEXT\fR]"
Save a profile.
.TP
.BI "remove " NAME
Delete a profile.
.TP
.B list
List all saved profiles.
.TP
.BI "show " NAME
Display a single profile.
.RE
.SS Bundles and utilities
.TP
.BR all " \fIHOST\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Run a full diagnostic bundle: lookup (full DNS), ping (4 packets),
trace, and TCP checks on ports 443 and 80.
Finishes with a unified one-page summary table.
.TP
.BR check " \fIFILE\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]"
Run many checks from a TOML (Python 3.11+) or JSON file in parallel
and exit 1 if any failed.
Supported types:
.BR ping ,
.BR tcp ,
.BR http ,
.BR tls ,
.BR lookup ,
.BR dnscheck ,
.BR health ,
.BR propagation ,
each with the same thresholds as the command
.RB ( max_loss ,
.BR max_latency ,
.BR expect_status ,
.BR min_days ,
.BR min_score ).
Saved profile names work as hosts.
.B "xping check \-\-example"
prints a commented starter file; see
.BR "SCRIPTING AND MONITORING" .
.TP
.B deps
Check whether the optional system binaries
.RB ( ping ,
.BR traceroute ,
.BR dig ,
.BR ss / netstat )
are installed, and print the correct package-manager install command
for the detected Linux distribution or macOS.
.TP
.BR completion " [\fISHELL\fR] [\fB\-\-install\fR|\fB\-\-uninstall\fR]"
Print a tab-completion script for
.I SHELL
.RB ( bash ,
.BR zsh ,
or
.BR fish ),
or set it up with
.BR \-\-install .
See
.B SHELL COMPLETION
below.
.TP
.B about
Display author name, version, license, source URL, and attribution
notice.
.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Global
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print
.I "xping <version>  by Mehdi Askari"
and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a help message for the selected command and exit.
.SS Output flags (all diagnostic commands; mutually exclusive)
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit machine-readable JSON (the full nested result) instead of
interactive terminal output.
.TP
.B \-\-csv
Emit CSV: one row per item (ping reply, hop, port, record, check \&...)
with a header line; results that are a set of fields (tls, whois,
http, health \&...) emit
.B field,value
rows.
.TP
.B \-\-markdown
Emit Markdown: a summary table followed by one table per section.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
No output at all; report the result only through the exit status.
.SS Address family
Accepted by
.BR ping ,
.BR trace ,
.BR mtr ,
.BR tcp ,
.BR portscan ,
.BR tls ,
.BR http ,
.BR health ,
.BR mtu ,
.BR osdetect ,
and
.BR all .
.TP
.BR \-4 ", " \-\-ipv4
Use IPv4 only.
.TP
.BR \-6 ", " \-\-ipv6
Use IPv6 only.
.PP
Without either flag IPv4 is preferred and IPv6 is used when the host
has no IPv4 address.
.SS ping
.TP
.BI \-c " N" ", \-\-count " N
Number of ICMP echo packets.
Default:
.BR 5 .
.TP
.BI \-t " SEC" ", \-\-timeout " SEC
Per-packet timeout in seconds.
Default:
.BR 2.0 .
.TP
.BI \-i " SEC" ", \-\-interval " SEC
Interval between packets in seconds.
Default:
.BR 0.5 .
.TP
.B \-\-watch ", " \-w
Continuous live ping mode. Displays a live sparkline with running
min/avg/max statistics. Press Ctrl-C to stop and print a final summary.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-loss " PCT"
Exit 1 if packet loss exceeds
.I PCT
percent (100% loss always fails).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-latency " MS"
Exit 1 if the average RTT exceeds
.I MS
milliseconds.
.SS trace
.TP
.BI \-m " N" ", \-\-max\-hops " N
Maximum number of hops.
Default:
.BR 30 .
.TP
.BI \-t " SEC" ", \-\-timeout " SEC
Per-hop timeout in seconds.
Default:
.BR 2.0 .
.TP
.BI \-p " N" ", \-\-probes " N
Probes per TTL value.
Default:
.BR 3 .
.TP
.B \-\-asn
Show the origin AS number and operator of every hop, looked up over DNS
via Team Cymru
.RI ( origin.asn.cymru.com ).
Opt-in because hop addresses are sent to Cymru's DNS servers.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-tcp
Probe with TCP connection attempts (SYN) instead of ICMP echoes.
A router whose TTL expires answers with ICMP time exceeded, quoting the
TCP header; the destination answers with SYN-ACK (open) or RST (closed),
and either counts as reached.
No root is needed: on Linux the router is read from the socket's error
queue
.RB ( IP_RECVERR ),
on macOS from an unprivileged ICMP socket.
Not available on Windows.
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
TCP port for
.BR \-\-tcp ;
implies
.BR \-\-tcp .
Default:
.BR 443 .
.SS mtr
.TP
.BI \-c " N" ", \-\-cycles " N
Number of ping cycles per hop.
Default:
.BR 10 .
.TP
.BI \-m " N" ", \-\-max\-hops " N
Maximum number of hops.
Default:
.BR 30 .
.TP
.BI \-t " SEC" ", \-\-timeout " SEC
Per-probe timeout in seconds.
Default:
.BR 2.0 .
.TP
.BI \-i " SEC" ", \-\-interval " SEC
Interval between cycles in seconds.
Default:
.BR 0.3 .
.TP
.B \-\-asn
Add an AS number/operator column (see
.BR trace ).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-loss " PCT"
Exit 1 if packet loss at the destination hop exceeds
.I PCT
percent.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-latency " MS"
Exit 1 if the destination's average RTT exceeds
.I MS
milliseconds.
.SS lookup
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-full
Also query TXT records (e.g. SPF).
.TP
.BI \-s " IP" ", \-\-server " IP
Query a specific DNS resolver instead of the system default.
Equivalent to
.BI "dig @" IP " RECORD HOST" .
Example:
.BR \-\-server\ 1.1.1.1 .
.SS tcp
.TP
.BI \-c " N" ", \-\-count " N
Number of TCP connection attempts.
Default:
.BR 3 .
.TP
.BI \-t " SEC" ", \-\-timeout " SEC
Per-attempt connection timeout in seconds.
Default:
.BR 2.0 .
.TP
.BI \-i " SEC" ", \-\-interval " SEC
Interval between attempts in seconds.
Default:
.BR 0.5 .
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-latency " MS"
Exit 1 if the average connect time exceeds
.I MS
milliseconds.
.TP
.BR \-\-watch ", " \-\-until\-up ", " \-\-every " \fISEC\fR"
Repeat the check; see
.B WATCH MODE
(default every 2 s).
.SS tls
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
TCP port to connect to.
Default:
.BR 443 .
.TP
.BI \-t " SEC" ", \-\-timeout " SEC
Connection timeout in seconds.
Default:
.BR 5.0 .
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-days " N"
Exit 1 if the certificate expires in fewer than
.I N
days (an expired certificate always fails).
.SS http
.TP
.BI \-t " SEC" ", \-\-timeout " SEC
Request timeout in seconds.
Default:
.BR 8.0 .
.TP
.BI \-\-expect\-status " CODE"
Exit 1 unless the final HTTP status is
.IR CODE .
Without it, any status \(>= 400 fails.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-latency " MS"
Exit 1 if the total request time exceeds
.I MS
milliseconds.
.TP
.BR \-\-watch ", " \-\-until\-up ", " \-\-every " \fISEC\fR"
Repeat the request; see
.B WATCH MODE
(default every 5 s).
.SS portscan
.TP
.BI \-p " LIST" ", \-\-ports " LIST
Comma-separated TCP ports and ranges.
Example:
.BR 22,80,443,8000-8010 .
Default:
.BR 1-1024 .
.TP
.BI \-t " SEC" ", \-\-timeout " SEC
Per-port connection timeout.
Default:
.BR 0.5 .
.TP
.BI \-w " N" ", \-\-workers " N
Concurrent port workers.
Default:
.BR 100 .
.TP
.B \-\-banners
Grab service banners from open ports.
Sends protocol-appropriate probes and fingerprints the response
against known signatures (SSH, HTTP, FTP, SMTP, Redis, PostgreSQL, etc.).
.SS ipscan
.TP
.BI \-t " SEC" ", \-\-timeout " SEC
Per-IP ping timeout in seconds.
Default:
.BR 1.0 .
.TP
.BI \-w " N" ", \-\-workers " N
Concurrent IP workers.
Default:
.BR 128 .
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Maximum IPs accepted from a CIDR or range.
Default:
.BR 4096 .
.SS sweep
.TP
.BI \-p " LIST" ", \-\-ports " LIST
TCP ports to probe on each host.
Default:
.BR 22,80,443 .
.TP
.BI \-t " SEC" ", \-\-timeout " SEC
Per host-port timeout.
Default:
.BR 0.5 .
.TP
.BI \-w " N" ", \-\-workers " N
Concurrent host workers.
Default:
.BR 128 .
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Maximum hosts from a CIDR or range.
Default:
.BR 4096 .
.SS mtu
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-mtu " BYTES"
Search ceiling for path MTU (bytes).
Default:
.BR 1500 .
Use
.B 9000
for jumbo-frame networks.
.TP
.BI \-t " SEC" ", \-\-timeout " SEC
Per-probe timeout.
Default:
.BR 2.0 .
.SS health
.TP
.BI \-c " N" ", \-\-count " N
ICMP ping packets used for scoring.
Default:
.BR 8 .
.TP
.BI \-t " SEC" ", \-\-timeout " SEC
Per-packet timeout.
Default:
.BR 2.0 .
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-score " N"
Exit 1 if the health score (0\(en100) is below
.IR N .
.TP
.BR \-\-watch ", " \-\-until\-up ", " \-\-every " \fISEC\fR"
Repeat the check; see
.B WATCH MODE
(default every 30 s).
.SS dnscheck
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-score " N"
Exit 1 if the DNS health score (0\(en100) is below
.IR N .
.SS propagation
.TP
.BI \-t " TYPE" ", \-\-type " TYPE
Record type:
.BR A ,
.BR AAAA ,
.BR CNAME ,
.BR MX ,
.BR NS ,
or
.BR TXT .
Default:
.BR A .
.TP
.BI \-\-expect " VALUE"
Exit 1 unless every resolver that answered returns
.I VALUE
(repeatable; MX values as
.RI \(dq PRIORITY " " HOST \(dq).
.TP
.BI \-s " IP" ", \-\-server " IP
Also query this resolver (repeatable).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-system
Skip your own system resolver.
.SS check
.TP
.BI \-w " N" ", \-\-workers " N
Checks run in parallel.
Default:
.BR 8 .
.TP
.B \-\-example
Print an example check file and exit.
.SS doctor
.TP
.BI \-\-port " PORT"
TCP port tested on
.IR HOST .
Default:
.BR 443 .
.SS net
.TP
.B \-\-no\-public
Skip the public IP lookup; nothing leaves the local network.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Also list interfaces that only have link-local addresses.
.SS speedtest
.TP
.BI \-c " N" ", \-\-connections " N
Parallel connections.
Default:
.BR 4 ;
use
.B 1
for a single stream.
.TP
.BI \-d " SEC" ", \-\-duration " SEC
Maximum download measurement time.
Default:
.BR 8 .
.SS listen
.TP
.BI \-\-proto " PROTO"
Filter by protocol.
Must be
.B tcp
or
.BR udp .
.
.SH WATCH MODE
.BR tcp ,
.BR http ,
and
.B health
accept:
.TP
.B \-\-watch
Repeat the check every
.B \-\-every
seconds until Ctrl-C, one line per check, highlighting DOWN / UP
transitions with the downtime, then print a summary (checks, uptime %,
state changes, longest outage, average latency).
The exit status reflects the final state.
.TP
.B \-\-until\-up
Repeat the check until it passes, then exit 0 \(em useful to wait for a
service in scripts (works with
.BR \-\-quiet ).
Ctrl-C exits 130.
.PP
Each run is judged like a normal run, so thresholds such as
.B \-\-max\-latency
or
.B \-\-expect\-status
also decide UP / DOWN.
Export formats cannot be combined with watch mode.
.
.SH SCRIPTING AND MONITORING
.RS
.nf
# fail a cron job / CI step when the link degrades
xping ping 1.1.1.1 -c 10 --max-loss 10 --max-latency 150 -q || alert

# wait for a database before running migrations
xping tcp db.internal 5432 --until-up -q && ./migrate

# certificate expiry alarm
xping tls example.com --min-days 14 -q || echo "renew soon"

# has the new DNS record propagated everywhere?
xping propagation example.com --expect 203.0.113.10 -q

# one config file, one exit code
xping check --example > checks.toml
xping check checks.toml --json > report.json
.fi
.RE
.
.SH OUTPUT COLOURS
.TP
.B Green
RTT < 30 ms \(em excellent latency.
.TP
.B Teal / Cyan
Informational labels and hostnames.
.TP
.B Amber / Yellow
RTT 30\(en300 ms \(em acceptable; or warnings in health/DNS checks.
.TP
.B Red
RTT > 300 ms, packet loss, certificate expired, or check failed.
.TP
.B Dim / Grey
Timeouts, closed ports, or informational context text.
.
.SH LIVE OUTPUT
Every ping reply, traceroute hop, TCP attempt, port result, and IP
scan result is printed immediately upon receipt \(em xping never
buffers results.
An animated spinner indicates an in-flight probe; it disappears and
is replaced by the result line the moment the response (or timeout)
is confirmed.
.PP
.B ping \-\-watch
redraws the current line in place (using ANSI cursor movement) so
the terminal does not scroll.
.B mtr
similarly redraws its hop table in place on every cycle.
.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
The Debian/Ubuntu package installs completion for bash, zsh and fish
automatically. For pip, pipx and source installs, run once:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xping completion \-\-install
.fi
.RE
.PP
This detects your shell from
.BR $SHELL ,
writes the script (zsh and bash:
.IR ~/.xping/completions/ ;
fish:
.IR ~/.config/fish/completions/xping.fish )
and adds a marked block that loads it to
.I ~/.zshrc
(zsh),
.I ~/.bashrc
(bash on Linux) or
.I ~/.bash_profile
(bash on macOS, whose Terminal opens login shells).
It is idempotent; re-run it after upgrading xping to pick up new commands
and flags, and name a shell to override detection
.RB ( "xping completion bash \-\-install" ).
.B xping completion \-\-uninstall
removes the script and the block.
.PP
To wire it up by hand instead:
.PP
.RS
.nf
source <(xping completion zsh)                       # ~/.zshrc
eval "$(xping completion bash)"                      # ~/.bashrc
xping completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/xping.fish
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use
.B eval
for bash: macOS ships bash 3.2, where
.B "source <(...)"
silently does nothing.
.PP
Completion covers commands and profile sub-commands, every option with
its description, option values with fixed choices (e.g.
.BR "listen \-\-proto" ,
.BR "propagation \-\-type" ),
hostnames and saved profile names wherever a host is expected, and
.B .toml
/
.B .json
files for
.BR check .
Options that cannot be combined (such as
.B \-\-json
and
.BR \-\-csv )
are not offered together in zsh.
.
.SH SAVED PROFILES
Profiles are stored in
.IR ~/.xping/profiles.json .
Any host-accepting command resolves a profile name transparently:
.PP
.RS
.nf
xping profile add prod-db 10.0.0.5 --port 5432 --note "Production DB"
xping ping prod-db
xping health prod-db
xping portscan prod-db --banners
.fi
.RE
.
.SH HEALTH HISTORY
Each run of
.B xping health HOST
appends a timestamped score entry to
.IR ~/.xping/health_history.json .
The last 10 scores are displayed as a sparkline with a trend arrow
(\(ua better, \(da worse, \(-> stable).
.
.SH PERMISSIONS
On macOS, and on Linux when the user's group is inside
.I net.ipv4.ping_group_range
(the default on most current distributions), xping uses unprivileged
ICMP sockets:
.BR ping ,
.BR trace ,
.BR mtr ,
and
.B health
need no root.
.PP
Otherwise native ICMP needs
.B root
or the
.B cap_net_raw
capability, and without it xping falls back to the system
.BR ping (8)
and
.BR traceroute (8)
binaries automatically.
.B xping deps
shows which mode is available.
To allow unprivileged ICMP sockets on a Linux system that disables them:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo sysctl -w net.ipv4.ping_group_range="0 2147483647"
.fi
.RE
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable all ANSI colour and style codes (see no-color.org).
.TP
.B XPING_DEBUG
Print full Python tracebacks on errors instead of the short
human-readable message.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The check passed.
.TP
.B 1
The check failed: host unresolvable or unreachable (100% loss), port
closed, HTTP status \(>= 400, expired certificate, DNS error, a
threshold option was exceeded (the reason is printed on standard
error unless
.BR \-\-quiet ),
any check in a
.B check
file failed \(em or an unexpected error occurred.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage: bad arguments, incompatible options, or an invalid
.B check
file.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted by the user (Ctrl-C / SIGINT).
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B xping 8.8.8.8
Auto-ping Google DNS and display follow-up command suggestions.
.TP
.B xping google.com
Auto-ping google.com (same shorthand, works with hostnames too).
.TP
.B xping doctor
Diagnose why the internet is not working, step by step.
.TP
.B xping doctor db.example.com \-\-port 5432
Check the basics, then whether db.example.com accepts connections on 5432.
.TP
.B xping ping google.com
Explicit ping with 5 packets.
.TP
.B xping ping 1.1.1.1 \-c 20 \-i 0.1
Twenty rapid-fire pings to Cloudflare DNS.
.TP
.B xping ping google.com \-\-watch
Continuous live ping with sparkline and running statistics.
.TP
.B xping trace 8.8.8.8 \-\-max\-hops 15
Trace the path to Google DNS, up to 15 hops.
.TP
.B xping trace example.com \-\-tcp \-\-port 443
Trace with TCP SYNs to port 443 when ICMP is filtered.
.TP
.B xping trace cloudflare.com \-\-asn
Trace with the network operator (AS number and name) of every hop.
.TP
.B xping ping \-6 google.com
Ping over IPv6.
.TP
.B xping mtr 1.1.1.1 \-\-cycles 20
MTR: per-hop live ping for 20 cycles with stdev and sparkline.
.TP
.B xping lookup github.com \-\-full
All DNS records including TXT/SPF for GitHub.
.TP
.B xping lookup github.com \-\-server 1.1.1.1
DNS lookup via Cloudflare instead of the system resolver.
.TP
.B xping dnscheck github.com
Full DNS health check: SPF, DMARC, DKIM, NS redundancy, MX backup.
.TP
.B xping rdns 8.8.8.8
Reverse-DNS lookup: resolve 8.8.8.8 to dns.google.
.TP
.B xping tls github.com
TLS certificate details, chain, expiry countdown, and lifetime bar.
.TP
.B xping http https://example.com
HTTP/2 detection, status, headers, redirect chain, DNS+TCP+TTFB timing.
.TP
.B xping whois cloudflare.com
WHOIS lookup with automatic RDAP fallback if port 43 is blocked.
.TP
.B xping tcp example.com 443 \-c 5
Five TCP connections to example.com on HTTPS port 443.
.TP
.B xping portscan example.com \-\-ports 22,80,443,8080 \-\-banners
Port scan with service banner grabbing on open ports.
.TP
.B xping ipscan 192.168.1.0/24
Discover live hosts in a local subnet.
.TP
.B xping sweep 192.168.1.0/24 \-\-ports 22,80,443
Find hosts exposing SSH/HTTP/HTTPS on a local network.
.TP
.B xping mtu 8.8.8.8
Binary-search the path MTU to 8.8.8.8.
.TP
.B xping health google.com \-c 16
Health score with 16 ping packets and trend history.
.TP
.B xping speedtest
Download/upload speed via Cloudflare with 4 parallel connections (no iperf3 needed).
.TP
.B xping net
Interfaces, gateway, DNS servers and public IPv4/IPv6 at a glance.
.TP
.B xping propagation example.com \-\-type MX
Compare MX answers across public resolvers.
.TP
.B xping tcp db.internal 5432 \-\-until\-up \-q
Wait until the database port accepts connections, then exit 0.
.TP
.B xping http https://example.com \-\-watch
Watch a website; highlight outages and print uptime on Ctrl-C.
.TP
.B xping check checks.toml
Run every check in a config file; exit 1 if any failed.
.TP
.B xping listen \-\-proto tcp
All locally listening TCP ports with process names and PIDs.
.TP
.B xping osdetect 192.168.1.1
Guess the OS of a local router from its ICMP TTL.
.TP
.B xping all cloudflare.com
Full bundle: lookup + ping + trace + TCP + unified summary.
.TP
.B xping profile add prod-db 10.0.0.5 \-\-port 5432
Save a profile named prod-db.
.TP
.B xping ping prod-db
Ping using the saved profile.
.TP
.B xping lookup github.com \-\-json > dns-report.json
Save DNS lookup results to dns-report.json.
.TP
.B xping completion \-\-install
Set up tab completion for your shell.
.TP
.B xping deps
Check which system tools are installed.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.xping/profiles.json
Saved target profiles (created automatically by
.BR "xping profile add" ).
.TP
.I ~/.xping/health_history.json
Health score history per host (appended by
.BR "xping health" ).
.TP
.I ~/.xping/completions/
Completion scripts written by
.BR "xping completion \-\-install" ,
which also adds a marked block to
.IR ~/.zshrc ,
.I ~/.bashrc
or
.IR ~/.bash_profile .
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/xping.1.gz
This manual page (installed by package managers).
.
.SH PRIVACY
Diagnostics contact the host you name. These commands also contact
third parties:
.B net
(1.1.1.1, for your public IP; skip with
.BR \-\-no\-public ),
.B speedtest
(speed.cloudflare.com),
.B doctor
(1.1.1.1, 8.8.8.8 and 9.9.9.9; captive.apple.com; www.cloudflare.com),
.B trace/mtr \-\-asn
(Team Cymru DNS; opt-in),
.B propagation
(six public resolvers),
.B whois
(IANA and registry WHOIS/RDAP servers), and
.BR lookup ,
.B dnscheck
and
.B rdns
(8.8.8.8, only as a fallback).
.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.B certifi
is the only required Python package and is installed automatically
with xping.
.PP
Optional system packages for full functionality:
.RS
.TP
.BR iputils-ping " (Debian/Ubuntu)  /  " iputils " (Fedora/Arch)"
Provides
.BR ping (8):
used by
.BR ipscan ,
.BR mtu
and
.BR osdetect ,
and as the fallback for
.BR ping ,
.B mtr
and
.B health
when no ICMP socket can be opened (always on Windows).
.TP
.B traceroute
Provides
.BR traceroute (8):
fallback for
.B trace
and
.B mtr
when no ICMP socket can be opened.
.br
Install:
.B sudo apt install traceroute
or
.BR "sudo dnf install traceroute" .
.TP
.BR dnsutils " (Debian/Ubuntu)  /  " bind-utils " (Fedora/Arch)"
Provides
.BR dig (1):
preferred for DNS queries (it honours your resolver configuration);
without it xping sends its own queries to 8.8.8.8 or the chosen
.BR \-\-server .
.TP
.BR iproute2 " (Linux)"
Provides
.BR ss (8):
used by
.B xping listen
to list open ports with process names.
.RE
.
